  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Brady Basso lasted 2.2 innings and Tampa Bay scored first and never trailed.",
    "what_this_means": "They left one runner on base in a scoring opportunity and didn't convert it. Seth Johnson, Drew Rom, Scott Blewett, and Hogan Harris combined for 3.7 scoreless innings across the back end. Johnson struck out four in 2.1 innings alone. The bullpen was clean. The problem was the other end. Tommy White went 3-for-4 with a home run and drove in one. The offense found eight hits but also eight strikeouts. Eight strikeouts is a lot when you're already chasing. They never led. They trailed after the first inning and never recovered from that moment. This is the shape of their losses now. Early deficit, bullpen survival, not enough from the bats when it mattered.",
    "what_to_watch": "They are now 61-91, 15 games back in a division that has moved on. The bullpen did not lose this game. The starter did not lose this game. The offense didn't do enough, and by didn't do enough, I mean they couldn't turn broad contact into pressure when they were behind. They had eight hits and eight strikeouts. Contact and emptiness in equal measure. One runner left on in a scoring situation is not stranded; it is conversion failure when everything else went quiet. White hit one worth remembering. The rest left the game early, mentally if not literally. A team that leaves early does not come back from 4-1 deficits against Tampa's closer. Clean bullpen arms do not matter when the deficit arrives in the first and the bats never threaten again. Eight hits. One run. That gap is the season.",
